  making the complete corresponding source code available under AGPL-3.0. This
  repository being fully public on GitHub satisfies that requirement.

Files included:
  - fastsam-s.onnx (fp32 ONNX export; input tensor `images` [1, 3, 1024, 1024]
    float32, values 0-1, letterboxed with pad value 114/255; output tensors
    `output0` [1, 37, 21504] (4 box (cx,cy,w,h in 1024-space) + 1 class-agnostic
    "objectness" score (sigmoid already applied in the graph — do not re-apply
    sigmoid to this value) + 32 mask coefficients per detection candidate) and
    `output1` [1, 32, 256, 256] (mask prototypes at a quarter of input resolution).
    Verified by loading the file with onnxruntime-node and inspecting
    input/output names, shapes, and score value ranges (bounded 0-1) directly
    (issue #50). This app's decoder
    (src/lib/modelLab/fastSam/postprocess.ts) reads outputs by shape, not by
    name, for the same defense-in-depth reason as YOLO11n-seg's decoder (a future
    re-export could use different output tensor names while keeping the same
    shapes).

Changes made:
  - No modification was made to the file contents. The file was copied verbatim
    from the upstream Hugging Face repository into public/models/fast-sam/ (renamed
    from `fastsam_s.onnx` to `fastsam-s.onnx` to match this repository's
    `public/models/<model-id>/` naming convention; the bytes are unchanged) so that
    smart-object-select can serve the model from its own origin instead of fetching
    it from the Hugging Face Hub at runtime.

Scope / interaction (see issue #50, parent #45):
  - Unlike YOLO11n-seg, FastSAM is a class-agnostic "segment everything" model: it
    has no notion of object categories (person/car/cat/etc.), only a single
    objectness score per detected instance. It cannot produce a human-readable
    class label for a detected instance.
  - Per parent #45's unresolved point that issue #50 was asked to settle: this
    integration uses the SAME interaction paradigm as YOLO11n-seg (issue #49) —
    full-automatic "segment everything" on image upload, followed by click-to-select
    among the already-detected instances — rather than FastSAM's native two-stage
    point/box-prompt pipeline (Ultralytics `FastSAMPrompt`). This keeps the Model
    Lab UI consistent across the two full-automatic-detection models and keeps the
    implementation scope smaller (no point-prompt-driven re-filtering to build).
